660 0 obj
<<
/Title (The meter readings are then joined with the building metadata using a left join on building_id. We chose a left join rather than an inner join deliberately: we wanted to keep all meter readings, even those for buildings whose metadata might be missing rather than silently dropping a potentially significant portion of the data.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 634 0 R
/Prev 659 0 R
/Next 647 0 R
>>
endobj
661 0 obj
<<
/Title (regression model. The Pipeline abstraction ensures that transformations learned during training \(the category-to-index mapping from StringIndexer, for example\) are consistently applied to the test set during evaluation, which is critical for avoiding data leakage.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 638 0 R
/Prev 651 0 R
/Next 662 0 R
>>
endobj
662 0 obj
<<
/Title (The VectorAssembler combines meter, log_sqft, and primary_use_idx into a single dense vector column called features, which is the format expected by all Spark MLlib estimators. The dataset was then split into a training set \(80%, 39,871 rows\) and a test set \(20%, 9,843 rows\) using a fixed random seed of 42 to ensure reproducibility.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 638 0 R
/Prev 661 0 R
/Next 652 0 R
>>
endobj
663 0 obj
<<
